    "https://www.tesu.edu/about/blog/2022/career-sites-to-kick-start-your-job-search.php": {
      "title": "11 General and Industry-Specific Career Sites to Kick-Start Your Job Search",
      "description": "11 General and Industry-Specific Career Sites to Kick-Start Your Job Search | Thomas Edison State University Skip to main content Return to home Higher Education. For Adults with Higher Expectations. Info For Prospective Students Current Students Military Students Community College Students Alumni Donors Corporate Partners | Request Info | Apply Now | myEdison | What are you looking for? Request Info | Apply Now | myEdison | What are you looking for? Search Degrees & Programs Degrees & Programs Find Your Program Courses Schools Why TESU? Why TESU? Save Time & Money Finish Your Degree Anywhere Generous Credit Transfer Career-Focused Programs Accreditations Hear From Our Grads Admissions Admissions Methods of Earning Credit Admission Requirements Transfer Pathways Steps to Apply Speak with an Admissions Counselor Apply Now Tuition & Financial Aid Tuition & Financial Aid Costs & Fees Financial Aid Scholarships Student Resources Student Resources Register for Courses Academic Advising Academic Calendars Student Accessibility Military & Veteran Resources Transfer Credits to TESU Career Development About TESU About TESU Our History Fast Facts Our Campus Leadership & Departments Read Our Blog Visit the Newsroom Events Info For Prospective Students Current Students Military Students Community College Students Alumni Donors Corporate Partners | Request Info | Apply Now | myEdison | Request Info | Apply Now | myEdison | 11 General and Industry-Specific Career Sites to Kick-Start Your Job Search December 30, 2022 Meg Frantz, MPSL '20 About TESU TESU Blog 2022 11 General and Industry-Specific Career Sites to Kick-Start Your Job Search I recently had cause to research websites that assist in the job search process. No, I’m not leaving as director of alumni engagement at Thomas Edison State University. This was for a class I took for my master's degree in public service leadership. During this course, what struck me was that each of my classmates brought different sources to the conversation making for a more comprehensive and thoughtful discussion. It amazed me that each of these websites brought something a bit different to the table while still delivering the support job seekers need to land their next job. Whatever the goal, these sites all presented various paths to get there. So I gathered the recommendations of my classmates, and a few of my own, to create a list of job search sites both general and industry specific that you can use for your next search. General Job Sites Careerbuilder.com The biggest of the job boards, it is searchable by almost any criteria to find locations, salaries and degree requirements that fit your professional goals. Post a resume for potential employers to view, read career-related articles and blogs, watch videos and explore different careers to learn more about the duties, expectations and salaries for different paths. Glassdoor.com This site is filled with company reviews to help you determine which companies may be the best fit for you. Explore salaries at different companies, test your skills with sample interview questions by topic or career path, and find out how your skills stack up by understanding your worth. Indeed.com This site collects listings from across the web and offers a streamlined process for searching through millions of jobs online. Dig into company reviews, upload a resume and compare company salaries. Monster.com As the name implies, it is the monster of job/career resources. Post a resume for employers and recruiters to review, read company profiles and find career advice resources in the form of blogs, videos and articles. It offers a cursory resume review for free, or you can pay a $189 fee for a full resume and cover letter review. It also provides access to more than 20,000 classes, which are free to start. ZipRecruiter Simply upload your resume and apply to jobs with just a click or tap, and then stay informed throughout the process with instant alerts whenever your application is reviewed. LinkedIn It’s not just for networking. Search open job postings and then use your extended network to learn about new opportunities. Join groups to participate in conversations and follow companies for which you’d like to work. SimplyHired Browse jobs by type, company, city, state or salary through the site’s aggregator as it scans through thousands of websites and listings in seconds to deliver the opportunities you’re looking for. Industry-Specific Job Sites Association of Clinical Research Professionals Designed for those working in the clinical research profession, this site offers job info, ways to connect with others in your field, course work to remain current and certification information. If you’re looking to launch your career in clinical research, the site also offers tips on getting started. Find virtual and in-person networking opportunities to build good connections for future opportunities. American Nurses Association (ANA) Career Center Nurses can find opportunities and search for positions by sector and discipline as well as geographic location. Create a job alert and monitor thousands of nursing positions and employers in all areas of healthcare delivered to your email inbox daily. Higher Education Recruitment Consortium (HERC) With more than 700 colleges, universities, hospitals, research labs, government agencies and nonprofit organizations within its consortium, HERC offers a large database of higher education and related jobs for academic, executive and staff/administrative positions. Job seekers can access job opportunities, create daily job alerts, and find career advice and regional resources by state for a career in academia. Society for Human Resources Management (SHRM) HR Jobs The Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM), the world’s largest HR professional society, provides a database of HR jobs across the country in popular specialties like benefits, compensation, employee relations, organizational development, training/development, recruitment and more. Apply and track your application through the SHRM job site while researching employers and salaries that match your career goals. Every site has its pros and cons. Some are comprehensive databases, while others provide you with an opportunity to learn where you stand with your current skills and education. Consider signing up for multiple sites to take advantage of the benefits that each offers. Whatever you do, automate your job search. Sign up for several sites and create a job alert that will notify you of new listings so you don’t find them after they close. You’ll be alerted immediately and can save yourself hours each week combing through every website. Found the Perfect Job Posting? Now What? You've bookmarked dozens of dream positions, set up job alerts, and polished your resume. But there's that one line in every posting that stops you cold: \"bachelor's degree required\" or \"master's preferred.\" Don't let a missing credential be the reason someone else gets YOUR dream job. While you're building that perfect job search strategy, why not also build the qualification that opens every door? Your experience is valuable, but paired with the right degree, it becomes unstoppable.The next time you see that perfect job posting, be ready to apply with confidence — not just hope. Stop watching opportunities pass you by and explore TESU’s degree programs today. To Top Request Info Find Your Program Apply Now Also of Interest: Job Hunting How to Combat Ageism in the Job Market When... Complete Guide to Career Networking Online 111 W. State St. Trenton, NJ 08608 admissions@tesu.edu 609-777-5680 Newsroom Events University Store Careers at TESU Contact Us Give/Donate ©Copyright 2026 All Rights Reserved Privacy Policy Policies & Procedures Compliance Info Web Accessibility Sitemap"
    },
    "https://www.tesu.edu/about/blog/2025/job-market-trends.php": {
      "title": "2025 Job Market Trends: Growing Fields and Required Qualifications",
      "description": "2025 Job Market Trends: Growing Fields and Required Qualifications | Thomas Edison State University Skip to main content Return to home Higher Education. For Adults with Higher Expectations. Info For Prospective Students Current Students Military Students Community College Students Alumni Donors Corporate Partners | Request Info | Apply Now | myEdison | What are you looking for? Request Info | Apply Now | myEdison | What are you looking for? Search Degrees & Programs Degrees & Programs Find Your Program Courses Schools Why TESU? Why TESU? Save Time & Money Finish Your Degree Anywhere Generous Credit Transfer Career-Focused Programs Accreditations Hear From Our Grads Admissions Admissions Methods of Earning Credit Admission Requirements Transfer Pathways Steps to Apply Speak with an Admissions Counselor Apply Now Tuition & Financial Aid Tuition & Financial Aid Costs & Fees Financial Aid Scholarships Student Resources Student Resources Register for Courses Academic Advising Academic Calendars Student Accessibility Military & Veteran Resources Transfer Credits to TESU Career Development About TESU About TESU Our History Fast Facts Our Campus Leadership & Departments Read Our Blog Visit the Newsroom Events Info For Prospective Students Current Students Military Students Community College Students Alumni Donors Corporate Partners | Request Info | Apply Now | myEdison | Request Info | Apply Now | myEdison | 2025 Job Market Trends: Growing Fields and Required Qualifications January 31, 2025 TESU About TESU TESU Blog 2025 2025 Job Market Trends: Growing Fields and Required Qualifications As we navigate through an increasingly digital world, the employment landscape continues to evolve at an unprecedented pace. For professionals considering their next career move, understanding these emerging trends isn't just helpful — it's essential for staying competitive. Technology and Data Revolution Data Science and Analytics: Turning Numbers into Knowledge Remember when making business decisions was primarily about intuition? Those days are firmly behind us. Organizations are actively seeking professionals who can transform raw data into actionable insights, with the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics projecting a robust 35% growth rate for data science roles through 2025. A bachelor's degree in data science or computer science provides the foundation for these roles, combining statistical analysis, programming and machine learning. The most successful professionals in this field are those who can bridge the gap between complex data and clear business insights. Software Development and Engineering Software development has become the backbone of modern business operations. While coding skills remain crucial, today's developers are more like digital architects, building the foundations of our increasingly connected world. A bachelor's degree in computer science prepares professionals for this evolving landscape, covering essential areas such as: Advanced programming concepts Software architecture Systems design Cloud computing solutions Cybersecurity fundamentals Educational Leadership and Innovation Education has moved far beyond traditional classroom walls. Modern educational leaders are pioneering new ways of learning that combine time-tested teaching principles with cutting-edge technology. A Master of Arts in Educational Leadership prepares professionals to: Design innovative curriculum solutions Lead technology integration initiatives Create inclusive learning environments Implement data-driven educational strategies Business and Financial Management Financial Leadership In an era where financial technology is reshaping traditional banking, a master’s degree in accounting has become more valuable than ever. Modern financial leaders need to be both number-savvy and tech-savvy, capable of: Navigating complex regulatory environments Implementing advanced financial technologies Managing digital transformation initiatives Developing robust risk management strategies Management and Leadership: Guiding Teams in a Digital Age The rise of remote and hybrid work environments has transformed how we think about leadership. A master’s in management equips professionals with the skills to: Drive strategic planning and execution Foster team collaboration across digital platforms Manage organizational change effectively Navigate global business operations Emerging Hybrid Roles One of the most intriguing developments in the job market is the rise of hybrid roles that combine multiple skill sets. These positions often require professionals who can wear multiple hats: Technical managers combining computer science expertise with leadership abilities Data analysts who understand both statistical modeling and business strategy Financial technology specialists merging accounting knowledge with digital innovation Educational technologists blending pedagogical expertise with technical skills Key Skills for Future Success Regardless of your chosen field, certain skills have become universally valuable and non-negotiable: Digital literacy and technology adaptation Data analysis and interpretation Project management Remote team leadership Change management Continuous learning ability Looking Ahead The job market of 2025 rewards professionals who combine deep expertise with adaptability. Whether you're pursuing a career in technology, education, finance or management, success increasingly depends on your ability to evolve with your industry. Formal education remains a valuable pathway for developing expertise in these growing fields. Programs that balance theoretical knowledge with practical application are particularly effective in preparing professionals for the challenges ahead. As organizations continue to navigate digital transformation and global challenges, the demand for qualified professionals who can adapt and grow will only increase. The key to success? Stay curious, keep learning and be ready to embrace change — your future self will thank you. Discover all there is to know about TESU, our programs and our services to help you make decisions about your future learning. Have questions? Request information from our team . To Top Request Info Find Your Program Apply Now Also of Interest: The Beginner’s Guide to Career Planning: Smart... How to Combat Ageism in the Job Market When... 5 Growing Homeland Security Careers for 2016 and Beyond 111 W. State St. Trenton, NJ 08608 admissions@tesu.edu 609-777-5680 Newsroom Events University Store Careers at TESU Contact Us Give/Donate ©Copyright 2026 All Rights Reserved Privacy Policy Policies & Procedures Compliance Info Web Accessibility Sitemap"
    }
